{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ROOT CAUSE CONFIRMED — deterministic reproduction, and the 14-minute gap explained.\n\nMECHANISM. CI runs the unit suite with `--timeout=300 --timeout-method=thread` (.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml). pytest-timeout's *thread* method calls `os._exit(1)` from its timer thread, so the xdist worker process dies outright rather than the test failing. Reproduced locally by changing ONLY the budget:\n\n    pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_wiki_render_hardening.py \\\n      -n 2 --dist worksteal --timeout=20 --timeout-method=thread\n\nemits `[gw0] node down: Not properly terminated` and then `worker 'gw0' crashed while running '...::test_rendered_output_is_byte_identical_to_the_landed_renderer'` — the same shape as CI, with no traceback and no assertion text.\n\nCOST. The test walks the whole committed corpus in ONE test function: 884 renderable units, each rendered twice (`_convert` plus the `_legacy_convert` oracle) = ~1768 serial pandoc spawns. Measured 91.6s idle; under saturating CPU pressure (2x hw.ncpu spinners — the fade-ea90 precedent) the SAME test measured 504.8s, i.e. 1.7x past the 300s guard. macOS is the scarcest cell in the runner pool, which is why only that cell reds.\n\nTHE 14-MINUTE GAP IS A REPORTING ARTIFACT, NOT KILL LATENCY. The two log lines are emitted by different parts of xdist at different times:\n- `node down: Not properly terminated` — xdist/workermanage.py:407, logged LIVE at the moment the worker dies.\n- `worker 'gwN' crashed while running ...` — xdist/dsession.py:436 (`handle_crashitem`), which builds an ordinary `pytest.TestReport(outcome='failed', longrepr=msg)`. Pytest prints failure reports in the FAILURES section at SESSION END, not inline.\n\nSo the elapsed time between the two lines is simply how long the surviving workers took to finish the rest of the suite — it is not evidence about how long the kill took. That fits the run arithmetic: the session totalled 1602.64s (26.7 min), the crash landed ~12.7 min in, and 14 min of suite remained. The same structure appears in my local repro, where the gap is small only because the session ended right after.\n\nBoth alternative branches are therefore not needed to explain the evidence, and neither is supported: the timeout demonstrably DOES fire on this path (the local repro kills the worker at the budget while the test is inside pandoc subprocess work), and the footprint is not an OOM candidate (the test holds ~884 short strings and spawns ONE pandoc at a time). Instrumented confirmation of both follows in the next comment.\n\nNot covered by the osprey sharing commit 48a3ba0b8a: that touched test_wiki_render_corpus.py only, and its `corpus_pass1` artifact holds pass-1 renders of whole bodies, not the per-unit `_convert`/oracle pairs this test compares. Siblings measured healthy post-48a3ba0b8a (worst call 12.5s), so this was the last test of the shape."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786653629163683001,"uuid":"8c2230eb-da6f-4369-aa15-3d51b015a7d3"}